{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>All these are the tribes of Israel, twelve.</b> The verse should have said אלה שבטי ישראל (these are the tribes of Israel), and the reason he mentioned כל אלה (all these) is because he rebuked Reuben, Simeon, and Levi and did not bless them — this verse teaches you that all of them are equal one to the other and worthy of blessing; thus explained Rashi, of blessed memory." "<b>And according to </b>my opinion, the verse alludes that all of existence in its entirety is sustained in the merit of the twelve tribes of Israel, and therefore it used concerning them the expression כל אלה (all these), like the expression used concerning all existent beings altogether (Isaiah 66:2): ״ואת כל אלה ידי עשתה״ — 'and all these My hand made' — and it comes to teach that all existent beings altogether are founded upon the twelve tribes of Israel. This is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: 'The thought of Israel preceded the world.' And therefore you will find the number twelve inscribed in all three parts of existence: in the supernal world — twelve angels surrounding the Throne of Glory, corresponding to what is written concerning the throne of Solomon (I Kings 10:20): ״ושנים עשר אריים עומדים שם על שש המעלות מזה ומזה״ — 'and twelve lions stood there on the six steps, on this side and on that'; in the middle world — twelve constellations; in the lower world — twelve tribes of Israel. And upon this Solomon said in his wisdom (Ecclesiastes 7:14): ״גם את זה לעומת זה עשה האלהים״ — 'also this one opposite that one Elohim made' — and understand this." "<b>And this is what their father spoke to them.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): its meaning is, 'and this is the blessing which their father spoke to them.'" "<b>And by way of </b>the Kabbalah, the word זאת (this) is Knesset Israel, of which is written (Song of Songs 7:8): ״זאת קומתך דמתה לתמר״ — 'this your stature is like a palm tree'; and it is the תרומה (offering), as is said (Exodus 25:3): ״וזאת התרומה״ — 'and this is the offering'; and it is the ברית (covenant), as is said (Genesis 9:12): ״זאת אות הברית״ — 'this is the sign of the covenant'; and it is the תורה (Torah), as is said: ״זאת התורה״ — 'this is the Torah' — which was given in the unique Name, as is said: ״וידבר אלהים״ — 'and Elohim spoke'; and to this the Prophet alluded, saying (Habakkuk 3:10): ״נתן תהום קולו״ — 'the deep gave forth its voice'; and this is what is written (Leviticus 16:3): ״בזאת יבא אהרן אל הקדש״ — 'with this shall Aaron come into the holy' — for this is the gate to HaShem through which the righteous enter into the holy, in the manner of what is said (Psalms 134:2): ״שאו ידיכם קדש וברכו את ה'״ — 'lift up your hands in holiness and bless HaShem.'" "<b>And behold,</b> Jacob attained this measure, and it descended with him to Egypt, and therefore the verse says וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם — that he rested this blessing upon them. And they said in the Midrash: 'וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם — Jacob said to them: a man like me is destined to bless you, and from the place where I stopped, from there he begins.' They alluded to us by this that Jacob attained only this last one, and therefore he mentioned it last and concluded his words with it. But Moses, who merited and attained higher than it, unto wisdom (חכמה) — therefore he began with this last one, and it was the beginning of his words, and from there he ascends and enters into the holy. And therefore you will find that Moses alluded to ten sefirot in that section, and there I shall explain this with the help of the Blessed One." "<b>Each according to his blessing he blessed them.</b> But did he not already mention ויברך אותם — 'and he blessed them'? <b>By way of the plain sense (peshat):</b> ויברך אותם he said in general; איש אשר כברכתו ברך אותם — in particular. Or one may say: ויברך אותם — for the hour; איש אשר כברכתו ברך אותם — for the generations." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> since he gave to Judah the might of a lion, and to Naphtali the swiftness of a gazelle, and to Benjamin the pouncing of a wolf, one might think that he did not include them in all the blessings — therefore Scripture says: ברך אותם, 'he blessed them.'" "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'Each according to his blessing he blessed them' — the twelve tribes are parallel to the twelve conduits above; and David alluded to them when he said (Psalms 122:4): ששם עלו שבטים שבטי יה — 'where the tribes would go up, the tribes of Yah.' He mentioned שבטים for the twelve above and שבטי יה for the twelve below, which are sealed with יו״ד ה״א at beginning and end — the Reubenite and the Simeonite. And this is what it says: איש אשר כברכתו ברך אותם — according to their blessing above, He blessed them below."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {7 "The phrase הראובני והשמעוני (literally 'the Reubenite and the Simeonite') is opaque in context; rendered literally without expansion. The reference to names 'sealed with yod-he at beginning and end' is left as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:05.341475455Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}